On June 7, Russian President Vladimir Putin signed the agreement to leave the Open Skies Treaty and informed the parties on June 18 that Russia will formally withdraw from the treaty within Next 6 months.
The Open Sky Treaty has been signed and implemented since 2002, allowing signatories to fly unarmed aircraft over each other's territories to monitor and collect data on military forces and related activities. And the military in the sense of building trust.
In November 2020, the United States under Donald Trump decided to withdraw from the Open Skies Treaty after accusing Russia of long-term violations of the treaty. Russia has denied the allegations and has decided to follow suit.
