(Phnom Penh): Mr. Ho Sethy, Director of the Cabinet of Samdech Akka Moha Sena Padei Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, on December 17, 2021, issued a statement on the publication that Mr. Kiep was named in a land dispute in the province. Mondulkiri.
He stated that he never knew the face and name of Teang Sotha or Sreng Vuthea and did not know the name of the accused landowner.
"I have great hatred for the wicked and filthy people who commit immoral acts by the window of the reputation of people they do not even know, and I strongly condemn them," he said. "And let the authorities take action according to the law in force against this lowly people seriously." The Royal Government of Cambodia Credit Guarantee Corporation provides 173 loan guarantees worth more than $ 20 million
(Phnom Penh): The Cambodian Credit Guarantee Corporation (CSC), a public enterprise under the technical and financial guardianship of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, has provided 173 loan guarantees totaling more than 20 million. Dollars as of December 10, 2021. This is according to the press release of the Cambodian Credit Guarantee Corporation.
The Cambodian Credit Guarantee Corporation is the first credit guarantee company in Cambodia to be established by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister, as a public enterprise under the technical and financial guardianship of the Ministry of Economy and Finance.
The main mission of the Cambodian Credit Assurance Corporation is to provide credit guarantees to participating financial institutions (PFIs) to support businesses that are able to repay but lack collateral when applying for a loan.
Seeing that credit guarantee services can contribute to economic recovery, the Cambodian Credit Guarantee Corporation on December 16, 2021 organized a workshop on "Credit guarantee services: benefits and opportunities to support the development of small and medium enterprises. »To collect the views of lenders and borrowers on this credit guarantee service.
The workshop was supported by the Ministry of Economy and Finance, the Asian Development Bank (ADB), and the United Nations Capital Development Fund (UNCDF), which brought together stakeholders including: the Royal Government, development partners, financial institutions, and Represent business owners to discuss the benefits and opportunities of using credit guarantee services to support recovery and business expansion.
All the speakers expressed the same view that the lack of collateral is one of the main challenges in obtaining a formal business loan.
The speakers also agreed that credit guarantee services could support banks and microfinance institutions to provide more loans to businesses, especially businesses that lack collateral.
In addition, the keynote speakers also discussed the efforts of relevant institutions to improve access to finance for small and medium enterprises in the context of economic recovery in Cambodia.
Discussions mentioned that the Royal Government has put in place a number of policies to support small and medium enterprises in accessing financing.
In addition, stakeholders have been involved in this mission by increasing financial literacy, developing innovative financial products, and improving the financial sector infrastructure.
Participants expressed their willingness to continue to support small and medium enterprises in accessing financial services. The workshop attracted more than 300 participants from various institutions such as the Royal Government, development partners, financial institutions and the business community.
