Dr. Sok Touch: Samdech Techo Hun Sen has enough experience to help negotiate internal problems of Myanmar
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Sok Touch, President of the Royal Academy, expressed his hope that as the ASEAN Chair in 2022, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, who has experience in resolving his civil war, will be able to help negotiate internal solutions. Of Myanmar.
During the internal turmoil of nearly a year, Myanmar, a young member of ASEAN, was absent from the 2021 ASEAN Summit after ASEAN chair Brunei Darussalam did not issue an invitation to the incumbent military leader. Attendance powers make ASEAN unable to find its own consensus.
According to Dr. Sok Touch, Cambodia has enough civil war experience to help negotiate a solution to Myanmar, especially the end of the civil war in 1998. In addition, around the gradual preparation of Cambodia to chair ASEAN in 2022, in which the new context that many countries are looking at is the issue of Myanmar and the South China Sea.
Dr. Sok Touch urges Western countries, superpowers, civil society organizations and political parties to set aside time for veteran politicians, including Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen, to negotiate and find solutions for Myanmar.
He observed that so far, Cambodia is under constant pressure in the social context, with allegations that Cambodia is helping to find a solution to the Myanmar problem from the Middle East and superpowers.
At the same time, he said that compared to the civil war experience between Cambodia and Myanmar, Cambodia experienced decades of war from 1965 to 1993, while Cambodia was able to successfully end the civil war in 1998.
Regarding the issue of Myanmar, Samdech Techo Prime Minister has recently said that he has coordinated as the chair of ASEAN with the task of ASEAN to return to the 10 ASEAN by 2022. International analysis: "Democracy" that the United States is forcing to practice abroad is just an excuse for hegemony
BEIJING, Dec. 13 (Xinhua) - Military personnel involved in a drone strike in Afghanistan have killed at least 10 civilians, the Pentagon said on Monday. That will not suffer any punishment.
A Pentagon spokesman said the issue was "just a matter of agenda, not the result of negligence."
On August 29, the US military launched a drone strike on a car in the Afghan capital, Kabul, claiming that it had eliminated the threat posed by Kabul International Airport. "Islam" in Afghanistan.
Faced with all sorts of suspicions from the international community, the US Pentagon has finally acknowledged that US troops did not actually shoot what they called "terrorists" in the airstrikes in Kabul. The Afghan capital on August 29 mistakenly killed 10 civilians, including seven children.
However, in August, after a bomb attack at Kabul International Airport, US troops began firing indiscriminately at civilians. The brutal violence that massacred innocent civilians in Afghanistan has not yet been brought to justice.
According to statistics, since 2001, the war in Afghanistan has killed a total of 244,000 people, including more than 71,000 civilians. Even in Kabul, the capital of Afghanistan, bombings and acts of terrorism are commonplace.
To date, the United States and NATO have withdrawn all troops from Afghanistan. This means that the 20-year-long war has ended abruptly, leaving only what is left of Afghanistan.
As a country with a history of more than 240 years, the United States has waged and participated in more than 200 wars. Just after the end of World War II from 1945 to 2001, out of 248 armed conflicts in 153 regions of the world, the United States instigated 201, or 81 percent of the total. Undoubtedly, America is the most war-addicted country in the world.
In addition, US war addiction is also economically viable. In 2019, Philippine President Duterte said in a speech that the United States expects war to continue because war will force other countries to buy aircraft, warships and ammunition from the United States until the war is over. Many will lose their jobs. It can be said that Duterte's words are very good.
