(Phnom Penh): The Education Workshop on the Prevention of Crimes in Southeast Asia is organized into three separate sessions for the end of 2021 and the beginning of 2022 by the Documentation Center of Cambodia and the Office of the United Nations Special Adviser (UN). On the prevention of genocide under the technical support of the United States Agency for International Development (USAID) and the United Nations Development Program (UNDP).
The first workshop, held on November 28, 2021, is an online workshop with the participation of participants who are history teachers from Cambodia, Vietnam and Thailand.
The second workshop, held in Cambodia from December 6 to 8, 2021, is a three-day workshop with the direct participation of Cambodian teachers from Tbong Khmum, Kandal and Prey Veng provinces.
The third workshop will take place from 13 to 15 December 2021 in Cambodia, with participants from Cambodia, Vietnam and Thailand.
The purpose of this workshop is to train teachers in Southeast Asia to better understand the definition of violent crime, the risk factors that lead to the occurrence of violent crime, and the importance of crime prevention.
Christopher Dering is an international trainer who presents and explains the definition of risk factors and how to prevent heinous crimes such as genocide, crimes against humanity and war crimes.
In this course, national and international trainers were divided into three groups for discussion, and each group mentioned one of the eight factors, including:
In this workshop, Him Huy, a former 703rd Brigade member who used to work in S-21, was invited to share his story from his recruitment to the Khmer Rouge in the liberated areas until the fall of the Khmer Rouge regime. On January 7, 1979.
A history teacher from Thailand asked Him Huy, "Why can you survive to this day?" How can today's youth react to the history of genocide? ” "I can live because I endure, I just want to live, I do not want to have a festival, and only education that young people can get involved, remember and prevent."
Sek Say, the daughter of Chan Kim Srun, a former victim at S-21 who was killed along with her 5-month-old daughter, recounted her story to remember about her mother.
The workshop provided participants with knowledge of the risk factors that lead to crime in Cambodia, Germany and Rwanda. In addition, all Cambodian teachers have gained new knowledge related to modern teaching methods to bring to teach their students by following the student center policy to increase knowledge and deepen students' thinking.
