Asian Development Bank (ADB) lowers Malaysia, Vietnam growth forecasts
The Nikkei Review reported, citing the latest ADB economic outlook, that the economies of Malaysia and Vietnam contracted in the third quarter after fighting the Delta virus. Both countries are now facing drastic cuts to their growth forecasts.
Malaysia's economy is now expected to grow 3.8 percent this year and 5.9 percent next year, lower than previous estimates of 4.7 percent this year and 6.1 percent. Next year. Vietnam's economic growth is expected to slow to 2% this year, compared with previous estimates of 3.8%, before recovering to 6.5% next year.
Kovid-19 outbreaks in the region have declined and vaccinations have risen sharply, but an increase in global cases threatens to revive the economy, the bank said.
Southeast Asia's economy is expected to grow 3 percent this year, up from a previously forecast 3.1 percent. Next year, the region is expected to achieve 5.1 percent economic growth, higher than the previous estimate of 5 percent.
The Philippine economy will grow 5.1 percent this year, compared with a previous estimate of 4.5 percent. The country is projected to grow 6 percent next year.
