The following is a statement from Sar Kheng: The United States has evacuated more than 900 of its citizens and permanent residents from Afghanistan since the end of its military service.
The United States has helped more than 900 of its citizens and permanent residents to leave Afghanistan and relocate to the United States since, the State Department said Monday. August 31, after US troops are withdrawn from there. This was released by Reuters on Tuesday, December 14, 2021.
The State Department said in a statement that the United States has helped 479 U.S. citizens and 450 legal permanent residents directly leave Afghanistan and relocate to the United States by the end of the year. August is coming.
The State Department added that it was in contact with some Americans who wanted to leave Afghanistan to prepare and travel documents needed to leave, but did not have a specific date.
The two-decade US presence in Afghanistan came to an end when more than 124,000 civilians, including Americans, Afghans and others, were evacuated from Kabul. In August 2021, after the Taliban took over Afghanistan.
However, at that time, there were still thousands of Afghans who had worked for the United States and were at risk of persecution by the Taliban, who had not yet had the opportunity to leave Afghanistan.
