The Government of Cambodia shares five priority tasks related to mobilizing resources to achieve the 2030 Agenda in the context of Kovid 19
(Phnom Penh): On behalf of the Royal Government of Cambodia, Senior Minister Ly Thuch, Chairman of the National Committee for ESCAP, said that under the leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Royal Government of Cambodia has shared five priority tasks related to the mobilization of resources that the Royal Government has been working on. Implemented to achieve the 2030 Agenda in the context of Kovid 19.
The workshop aims to bring together experts and policymakers from least developed countries and developing island states in the Asia-Pacific region to discuss resource mobilization to achieve sustainable development goals and share experiences. In an effort to mobilize resources to achieve sustainable development goals.
The five priorities include:
First, turn the threat of the spread of Kovid 19 into an opportunity for each country's domestic reforms to strengthen economic competitiveness, create a favorable business environment and strengthen government revenue collection.
Second, to promote trade policy and trade facilitation, as well as to provide concessions to developing countries to ensure that the benefits of globalization are shared equitably, transparently and effectively.
Third, continue to strengthen financial stability, diversification and innovation, as well as enhance the role and dynamics of the private sector in meeting financing needs to achieve sustainable development goals by providing incentives aimed at promoting private investment and capital inflows. Hold developing countries, especially the promotion of foreign direct investment and public-private partnership mechanisms.
Fourth, ensure continued international assistance, especially financial assistance to developing countries, towards the sustainability of the momentum in the reform for the 2030 Agenda.
Fifth, continue to strengthen the ownership of developing countries by building partnerships between all development partners, including bilateral, multilateral, private and other sectors. In addition, we must ensure transparency and accountability between the policies of development partners, both bilateral and multilateral.
In addition, Senior Minister Ly Thuch thanked the participants from countries in the Asia-Pacific region and to UN ESCAP for organizing this workshop and always supporting the development in Cambodia.
He stressed that Cambodia is ready to join regional countries and development partners in efforts to mobilize resources and rehabilitate the socio-economic situation in order to continue to achieve a common vision for sustainable development. .
It should also be noted that the outcome of this regional workshop will contribute to filling the knowledge gap and as an input for the region, as well as sharing information on forms of global development, such as: Developments in the 1940s. And future initiatives.
