Lt. Gen. Virak Chhay Summeta's statement was made during a recent meeting with Dr. Sok Sokretya in Bangkok, Thailand. The above meeting was held to strengthen the friendship and solidarity between the Royal Government of Cambodia and the Thai Police.
Discussions focused on security management, cross-border trade, migrant workers. In particular, prevent illegal workers and online fraud. During the meeting, Lt. Gen. Virak Chhay Summetta thanked Dr. Sok Sokretya for his honor and for visiting the Thai Police Headquarters.
On behalf of the Royal Government of Cambodia, Dr. thanked the Thai police for their continuous crackdown on illegal brokers and the Thai police's good cooperation in tackling and ending cross-border crime. Better. At this point, it shows that the Royal Government of Cambodia is not involved and has never supported human trafficking or illegal labor trafficking.
During the meeting, Dr. Sok Sokretya requested the Thai side to continue to promote good cooperation in the fight against false information, the suppression of human trafficking crimes, the integration of the Mobile App, the safety of migrant workers with the Thai police system, and the prevention of opposition groups using social media. From Thailand to attack the leaders of the Royal Government of Cambodia, as well as to fight drug trafficking crimes along the Cambodian-Thai border.
In response, Lt. Gen. Virak Chhay Suthmeta, Deputy Commissioner General of the Thai National Police, made it clear that the policy of the Royal Government of Thailand, as well as the Thai police, should not allow opposition groups to use Thai territory as a platform for political action against any government. One. He added that the Thai police at all levels will continue to cooperate well with the Cambodian police to prevent activities against the Royal Government of Cambodia.
This working meeting was considered as a good relationship and promised to continue the close and stronger cooperation in cracking down on criminal groups along the Cambodian-Thai border.
The US Department of Labor released data showing that the consumer price index (CPI) rose 6.8 percent in November 2021 from a year earlier, Bloomberg reported. The closely monitored commodity price index rose just 0.8 percent in October, higher than forecast and indicates that the strong inflation trend that started this year will continue.
The rise in the consumer price index reflects the rise in prices of all types of goods. Gasoline, food, cars are all among the most expensive commodities and have contributed significantly to the rise in inflation.
The data also reinforces expectations that the Fed will step up the cancellation of its bond-buying program at its final meeting in 2021 next week.
Countries' central banks and politicians around the world have come under increasing pressure to tackle rising inflation as workers spend more on commodities and gasoline.
