The Buddha's palace is 3,700 meters above sea level and covers a total area of 360,000 square meters, while the construction area of the palace is 130,000 square meters. The largest monument is 117 meters high and has 13 floors from the foot of the mountain to the top of the mountain, including the palaces of Taling Temple, the Mahayana Buddhist Temple, the Dharma Temple, the residence of the monks, as well as the courtyard of the garden.
As a sacred place of Tibetan Buddhism, this Buddhist palace is visited by countless pilgrims and tourists every year. They usually start from the unmarked stone carvings at the foot of the mountain, across the escalator to the east gate.
In 1994, the Tibetan Buddhist Monument, or Pothala, was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List.
