This morning, Cambodia received an additional 300,000 doses of Sinovak vaccine with Kovak assistance.
The Kovak Center is an international mechanism established under the auspices of the World Health Organization and international development organizations to find 19 Kovid vaccines for poor and least developed countries.
Cambodia is also one of the countries designated by the Kovak Center to provide vaccine assistance to help fight 19 Kovid. The Kovak Center has set a prevalence of approximately 6.6 million doses of Kovid 19 vaccine to reach Cambodia in the first phase.
So far, more than 3 million doses of the vaccine have been delivered to Cambodia through the Kovak Center. The vaccines that Kovak helped bring to Cambodia include Sinovak, Astrageneka, Modena and Johnson & Johnson.
In relation to the Kovid 19 vaccine, Cambodia is a country that has found a quick vaccine for vaccination. Through a clear plan, both with the purchase of aid from friendly countries and the Kovak Center, Cambodia has so far received a total of more than 40 million doses of the vaccine.
With the first and second doses given to more than 14 million people, almost complete and continue to give the third dose to the people, now Cambodia has more than 6 million doses of vaccine.
The power of the vaccine, which the Royal Government of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, strives to deliver to the people, has saved Cambodia from the risk and epidemic of 19 Kovid and has reopened social activities.
During the same period, the ASEAN country also found five new deaths from the deadly disease, bringing the total death toll nationwide to 794, with 19 cases of Kovid infection across the country. The country increased to 273,362 cases.
Of the above new cases, 17 were imported cases, and all of them were domestic cases. Ho Chi Minh City, the epicenter of the outbreak, was responsible for 1,216 new cases today, while Hanoi, the second hotspot, reported 980 cases.
In a report on Sunday, the Vietnamese Ministry of Health also announced an additional 228 new cases of Kovid 19.
