To help boost the growth of the agricultural sector is indispensable from the lending of banking and financial institutions.
Banks and financial institutions have been actively involved with the agricultural sector. As of August 2021, banking and financial institutions have provided loans to the agricultural sector of about 17 trillion riels, equivalent to about 4.2 billion US dollars.
Although access to agricultural credit from financial institutions has been steadily increasing, challenges such as scarcity of collateral, high interest rates, complicated procedures, access to loans and limited space have been reported. And planting plans, while some people have not yet sought a loan from a financial institution, they still use the fertilizer of the seller before receiving the sale money to pay back.
To help encourage farmers to use credit as capital for agricultural development, financial experts offer three key pieces of advice.
Mr. Rom Ratha, Commercial Director of Amret Microfinance Institution, said, "In order to get low-cost capital, there are three main things: first, farmers must have a clear planting plan; second, there must be a record of income and expenses. Correct and third: try to build a reputation for repaying credit. "If you have a good credit history, clear records and a clear business plan, of course, credit terms and credit rates are cheaper than those without a clear plan."
Mr. Dok Loun, Vice Chairman of the Board of the Cambodian Agricultural Community Coalition, which has 1,200 farming community members, equivalent to more than 100,000 farming families, said that access to credit for farmers still has some challenges due to farmers and farming communities. Knowledge of the financial and banking sectors is still limited, so they are not prepared to take out loans from financial institutions.
