As for Sam Rainsy's warning about campaigning for the government to revoke the Cambodian American citizenship in support of the Cambodian People's Party, Ms. Ok Sokna and her husband were not interested. She and her husband believed that what Sam Rainsy was saying was impossible.
Ms. Ok Sokna emphasized, "For me, do not keep his words in my ears because I know it is impossible. "He spoke to me and I felt it was provocative, making me think that his politics were provocative and that this could not happen for Cambodian Americans."
According to Ms. Ok Sokna, just supporting a political party, there is no reason for the United States to revoke citizenship. Except for acts that support terrorism or commit acts that seriously affect the United States.
Keo Vanna is another Cambodian-American living in Kentucky who has lived in the United States for 17 years. Mr. Keo Vanna decided to support the Cambodian People's Party and took part in activities to help the ruling party in the United States.
Mr. Vanna decided to support the Cambodian People's Party because he saw that Cambodia under the leadership of the Cambodian People's Party has progressed steadily. He pointed out that when he returned to visit Cambodia from year to year, he saw that Cambodia was different from year to year.
According to Vanna, the activities of the Cambodian People's Party in the United States have never been banned by any authority.
"It simply came to our notice then, because this is our right," he said. The United States is a democracy, the father of democracy, so it means that both you and I have the right to choose. "Choose to love, support any party, they do not force us."
Since the day Sam Rainsy issued the warning, no action plan has emerged as a threat or concern to Cambodian-Americans.
