(Phnom Penh): More than 100 families affected by house fires in Daun Penh and Russey Keo districts received donations from the Cambodian Red Cross through the Red Cross Sub-Committee of each Khan.
In which, the CRC Sub-Committee of Russey Keo District organized the distribution of emergency relief supplies of the Cambodian Red Cross with Samdech Kittiprittbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen as the Chairman to the fire victims to facilitate the lives of 27 more families in Group 5, Village 3, Sangkat Chrang Chamres I, Khan Russey Keo, on the afternoon of December 10, 2021.
For Russey Keo district, the ceremony was presided over by Mr. Chin Bunthoeun, Director of the Cambodian Red Cross Branch in Phnom Penh, Mr. Ek Khun Doeun, District Governor and Mr. Sou Sokunthan, Deputy District Governor and Chairman of the CRC Sub-Branch Committee. Ms. Phnom Penh Branch of Cambodian Red Cross Branch, CRC Sub-Branch Working Group and Sangkat CRC Working Group.
Separately for Daun Penh district, it was chaired by Mr. Chin Bunthoeun, Executive Director of the Cambodian Red Cross Branch in Phnom Penh, representing Mr. Khuong Sreng, Governor of Phnom Penh and Chairman of the Committee of the Cambodian Red Cross in Phnom Penh, with the participation of Mr. Sok Penh Vuth, Governor of Daun Penh District and Honorary President of the Cambodian Red Cross Sub-Branch in the Royal Railway Campus.
Please be reminded that on December 10, two fires occurred almost simultaneously in Sangkat Srah Chak, Khan Daun Penh and Sangkat Chrang Chamres I, Khan Russey Keo, Phnom Penh, in which almost a total of people's houses were destroyed. During the rescue operation, the police used almost 200 cars to fully control the water.
In the first fire, about 80 houses were burnt, causing complete damage at an unregulated construction site along the railway line in Srah Chak commune, Daun Penh district due to a power outage. In the second case, 17 houses were destroyed by fire, causing complete damage to the unregulated construction site along National Road 5 in Sangkat Chrang Chamres I, Khan Russey Keo. Each.
Colonel Prohm Yorn, Director of the Fire Prevention and Extinguishing Office of the Phnom Penh Municipal Police, confirmed that for the rescue operation, the specialized force used 48 vehicles of the Fire Prevention and Extinguishing Office and used 98 trucks of water.
