Chinese central bank tightens control over yuan appreciation
BEIJING: China's central bank is trying to curb the yuan's appreciation after the currency hit a two-and-a-half-year high against the dollar. According to the AP.
Commercial banks in China on Thursday were ordered to increase the amount of their foreign currency reserves kept in reserve, the Associated Press reported. This is the second installation this year. The increase will reduce the amount of foreign currency available for trading in the market, making it easier for Beijing to manage the exchange rate.
The yuan has strengthened nearly 2 percent against the dollar since mid-August, pushing the exchange rate to 6.3762 to the dollar this week, a record high, according to the Associated Press. Most from May 2018.
An order to increase the amount of foreign currency deposits issued on Thursday increased the commercial bank's foreign exchange reserves from 7 percent to 9 percent. In May this year, the amount of foreign currency deposits was only 5 percent. Economists said at the time that the foreign exchange reserves could be around $ 20 billion.
The Chinese Communist Party said in 2015 that the yuan would be made a widely traded and free-to-use currency by 2020. But the currency is still dominated by concerns about sharp fluctuations in exchange rates and inflows into the world's second-largest economy.
A stronger yuan threatens to make Chinese exports more expensive in foreign markets, weighing on the recovery of the manufacturing sector after a slump last year. A stronger yuan will make imports of Chinese oil, iron ore and other raw materials cheaper for Chinese manufacturers. According to the AP news agency.
