2021 Census Results: Cambodia Provides Strong Habitat for White-throated Trout
The population of this species declined sharply in the late 20th century. Today, the worldwide population of white-tailed deer is estimated at just over 1,000, of which more than 670 are adult animals.
The Ministry of Environment has been collaborating with partner organizations in the conservation of Cambodian oysters, starting with the annual white-tailed deer census at four key locations since 2009. Preliminary census results show that there were only 310 white-tailed deer.
It should also be noted that according to the above census results, the Siem Pang Wildlife Sanctuary is an important habitat for white-tailed deer, recording a total of 373 animals. Sambo Wildlife Sanctuary in Kratie Province is the next most important location, recording 252 animals. In particular, other wildlife sanctuaries have also shown increased results.
On behalf of the Ministry of Environment, I would like to thank all the partners in the protection of the white-tailed deer and call on all people to participate in the protection and conservation of these rare animals, because it is of great value for biodiversity and global conservation and has "Prices for ecotourism attract tourists and income to the community and the national economy."
Mr. Seng Teak, Country Director of WWF-Cambodia, highlighted: “I welcome and applaud the partnership for the 2021 National Census, which demonstrates the positive steps of the joint efforts of the Ministry of Environment and "All partners in the protection of white-tailed deer and in reversing the decline of this endangered species in the future."
