Climate change makes harvested mangoes less productive, but prices skyrocket to 4,000 riel per kilogram
(Phnom Penh): Climate change remains a concern in Cambodia's mango harvest, which has reduced exports to China by the end of the year. It is expected that by the end of November 2021, more Cambodian mangoes will be exported to China, but weather conditions have hampered mango exports to China.
The tycoon said the sky continued to rain, making it difficult to harvest during October and November. Therefore, the yield for good quality packaged mangoes or blue mangoes for export is still small at the end of this year, but exports will increase in early January 2022 due to January, the time of mangoes. Harvest season begins.
Mr. Oknha continued that in some areas, it still rained until the beginning of the first week in December, making it very difficult to harvest fresh mangoes, the first quality.
"Due to the weather, the price of packaged mangoes has dropped a lot, but the price has risen sharply. Previous forecasts that the price of packaged mangoes in November and December was only 1,500 riel to 2,500 riel," he said. Per kilogram, but the actual price now jumps to 3,000 riel - 4,000 riel per kilogram.
The tycoon added that this price problem does not occur only in Cambodia, in Vietnam, Thailand and China are all facing the problem of this harvest, making the price of mango higher.
She said that for the farming community of Svay Keo, Turmeric, Kirirom, this year harvested about 70 percent and get a high price.
Please be informed that Cambodian fresh mango is the second fresh fruit after fresh banana which can be exported directly to China.
Cambodia has 130,000 hectares of mango plantations, more than 90,000 hectares of mango harvest with a yield of more than 15 tons per hectare, equivalent to a total yield of nearly 1.4 million tons per season.
