US Trade Agent Urges US President to Continue Withdrawing Tariffs on Imported Solar Under Tram Administration
The US Commerce Agency is recommending a four-year plan to extend the tax on solar components, dropping it to 0.25 percent a year from February next year. The recommendation comes weeks after it was determined that solar imports remain a threat to US solar manufacturers.
The US president will have to balance the contradictory goals. While promoting greater clean energy use is one of his key policy objectives, he is also working to boost the economy, including creating jobs in domestic production. The tax deduction benefits local solar panel manufacturers in the United States, but these taxes add to the cost of companies that install imported solar panels.
Mr. Tram imposed the tax in 2018. Although US solar production has risen slightly in his mandate, China is still the world's largest producer of solar. Most of the solar panels used in the United States are imported from Thailand, Malaysia and Vietnam.
The rejection is a victory for the American Solar Energy Industry Association, the largest solar business body in the United States, which has suggested that imposing tariffs on the three countries will affect the energy sector, which is critical to meeting the administration's goals. Of Joe Biden to promote clean energy and fight climate change. US solar manufacturers rely on low-cost imported products to make their projects more competitive.
