The Nicaraguan Ministry of Foreign Affairs announced on Thursday, "Today, the Government of the Republic of Nicaragua has severed diplomatic ties with Taiwan and will no longer have normal relations or official relations. Nicaragua recognizes that there is only one China in the world. "The People's Republic of China (PRC) is the only legitimate government that represents all of China, and Taiwan is an inseparable part of Chinese territory."
The non-recognition of Taiwan has reduced the list of countries with diplomatic relations to only 14 countries. Nicaragua's decision comes after months of strained relations between the Central American country and the United States.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra: Plastic project to exchange rice on Tonle Sap Lake in Siem Reap province has reduced the pollution of Tonle Sap Lake by plastic and protected the environment and biodiversity
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment, on December 9, 2021, highly appreciated that the plastic rice exchange project implemented since 2018 on the Tonle Sap Lake in Siem Reap province with the people in the communities has been reduced. Plastic pollution of the Tonle Sap Lake, as well as contributing to the protection of the environment and the protection of biodiversity in the Tonle Sap Lake, the largest freshwater lake in Southeast Asia.
Each year, the project reduces pollution by about 100 tonnes of plastic, both wet and dry, to the Tonle Sap Lake. Currently, the project has been expanding the clean-up of plastic waste in the environment that flows from various parts of rivers and streams during the flood season.
To date, more than 610 families in the Tonle Sap community have traded plastic for rice and other food items.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that the plastic rice exchange project is a project co-sponsored by the Ministry of Environment with the Tampaing Snong Russey Foundation, an organization implementing projects within the framework of Siem Reap province with communities living in the Tonle Sap Lake area. .
He said that in each community, there are plastic barns to store plastic collected from various areas in the community as plastic waste because people in the community use as well as collect in the Tonle Sap Lake area.
Regarding this project, the Secretary of State stated that the main purpose of establishing a plastic barn as well as the implementation of this plastic rice exchange project in one direction is to reduce the use of plastic or reduce pollution in the Tonle Sap Lake. Contribute to biodiversity in the Tonle Sap Lake, especially fish and other biodiversity.
In connection with this rice exchange plastic, people who participate in sorting plastic waste at home, which is household waste and participate in cleaning plastic in the environment will be rewarded with 10 kg of rice, along with some food items. Also.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that the implementation of this project is to encourage community members to participate in preventing the use of plastic, as well as to prevent the dumping of plastic bags across the field. The project was a resounding success, and people living in the communities happily and understandingly participated.
On that occasion, he also said that the plastic waste collected through the plastic rice exchange project will be used to produce bricks for school grounds and other constructions. The Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment has called on people not to burn plastic waste without proper techniques, as burning plastic waste will pollute the environment and if thrown into the water will pollute the water and affect the beauty of the Tonle Sap Lake. Of tourist destinations.
Sea Sophea Bamboo Foundation President Mr. Sea Sophal said that the plastic rice exchange project will continue to encourage and encourage the community to participate in cleaning plastic waste and properly separating plastic waste, not to burn and throw in Tonle Sap water.
At the same time, the project to clean plastic from the Tonle Sap Lake will also attract more tourists to create jobs and provide income to the people by watching the lives and livelihoods of the people on the Tonle Sap.
