South Korea urges teens to get vaccinated against Kovid 19 amid rising infections
SEOUL: South Korean health authorities on Thursday asked teens to be vaccinated against COVID-19 amid an increase in cases among them. This is according to the release by the Chinese news agency Xinhua on Thursday, December 9, 2021.
The South Korean Disease Control and Prevention Agency (KDCA) said in a statement that it strongly supports the vaccination of adolescents as the number of cases continues to rise among those who have not been vaccinated. Vaccines.
The total number of cases among adolescents aged 12 to 17 has risen from 3,630 in September to 4,837 in October and will continue to rise to 6,612 in November 2021. . Infection among secondary school students has increased due to their lower immunization rates compared to high school students, KDCA added.
According to the latest data, the full immunization rate among those aged 16-17 in South Korea is 65.8%, while among those aged 12-15 years only. The full vaccination rate among those aged 18 and over rose to 91.9 percent as of midnight Wednesday.
