Ministers in charge of Islamic Affairs of the five countries meet annually to strengthen ties, solidarity and exchange experiences on religious harmony
The Cambodian side was also present at the meeting with Mr. Yop Ascari, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Cults and Religions in charge of Islamic Religious Affairs, Mr. No Matsat, Undersecretary of State of the Ministry of Cults and Religions, Mr. Las Razak, Undersecretary of State of the Ministry of Commerce, and representatives of the leadership. High of Cambodian Islam as well.
The 19th Informal Annual Meeting of Ministers for Religious Affairs of Brunei, Indonesia, Malaysia, Singapore, abbreviated MABIMS was established on 7 August 1989 in Brunei, three countries: Brunei, Indonesia and Malaysia. 1992.
The purpose of MABIMS is to strengthen the relationship of unity, harmony, religion, well-being, exchange of experiences between Ministers in charge of Islamic affairs, general issues of religion and interests, cooperation, humanitarian work, etc. without interfering in the political affairs of member countries.
The meeting was held to change the co-ordinator, who has a two-year term, chaired by Indonesia for 2021-2022. There are three Malay-speaking leaders for each country. At the same time, MABIMS has established the Supreme Secretariat (SOM), which has four members for each country, as the Secretary of the Ministry of Religious Affairs or the Islamic Supreme Institution, meeting once a year.
The Chinese embassy urged Australia to take concrete steps to create favorable conditions for the promotion of bilateral ties. The Chinese embassy made the remarks just hours after the Australian government announced it had decided not to send officials to the Beijing Winter Olympics in February 2022.
Australia's diplomatic boycott of the Beijing Winter Olympics comes after the United States announced it would not send officials to the Games. However, the Chinese Embassy in Australia wished all Australian athletes success in the competition.
"As Cambodia's long-term development partner, ADB is committed to supporting the country's economic competitiveness and environmentally friendly development," Gill said. "These projects will promote the recovery of the Cambodian economy by improving health care services, the financial environment, infrastructure and urban services in some strategic locations and road networks."
The viable urban investment project will help provide better wastewater and solid waste management services for more than 140,000 people in Bavet, Kampot and Poipet. It will help the three cities improve their drainage and sewerage systems and reduce flooding. JFPR has provided $ 2 million in technical assistance to support efforts to improve land use planning and governance, as well as other work.
The second phase of the road network improvement project will rehabilitate 48 km of national and provincial roads in Prey Veng and Kandal provinces to promote economic development along the Greater Mekong Subregion Economic Corridor. The project will incorporate disaster and climate resilient design and assist the Ministry of Public Works and Transport in better road management.
An integrated financial sector development program will help the government expand access to credit among small and medium enterprises, implement reforms to promote financial stability and improve financial infrastructure.
The ADB is currently implementing a total investment project worth $ 2.08 billion in Cambodia. In 2020, ADB provided $ 477.2 million in loans and grants to Cambodia and an additional $ 281.3 million through co-financing.
ADB reserves for 2021-2024 include $ 1.38 billion in concessional loans and $ 36.8 million in grants to support Cambodia's economic development and recovery from the Kovid 19 pandemic.
