Research and investigation is still ongoing to gather more data, but it will also take some time. As a result, the world is facing a complex problem: if it chooses to wait until official data is available to make a decision, it may be too late to put policy into effect.
But if the current repressive measures are chosen, the policies that are put in place may have more opportunities to reduce the risk, or in other words, "prevention is better than cure."
The question of how to deal with ambiguity is not a scientific issue but an ethical one. The immediate introduction of health measures will surely affect and weigh on individual freedoms and well-being, while travel bans will hurt the economy as well as undermine international solidarity.
Scientifically, taking timely action may keep us safe, but it can also affect moral values. But if action is delayed, it could cause huge losses in the event that the new Omicron mutation virus enters the country unchecked, leading to an increase in cases.
