Evergrande's hard-earned efforts to meet its debt obligations have raised concerns about China's real estate sector, a key sector of the world's second-largest economy.
The grace period for repayment of two debts worth $ 82.5 million is set to end next Monday and could be the company's first default, the AFP news agency reported.
Evergrande is one of China's leading real estate companies and has slumped after Beijing introduced measures last year to curb speculative sales and over-indebtedness in real estate development. Of companies in this sector, which causes cuts in the source of cash for the company. But there are also signs that the government is beginning to ease restrictions.
The seven-member Risk Assessment Commission will be composed of only two senior officials from Evergrande, the AFP news agency reported. Other members from state institutions have raised speculation that the government could get involved in managing the company's $ 300 billion debt.
