International Analysis: What does Putin's visit to India mean in world politics? (Video inside) (New Delhi): Russian President Vladimir Putin's visit to India on Monday to meet with Prime Minister Narendra Modi has been reminiscent of the Cold War era. At a time when relations between Moscow and New Delhi are stronger than ever.
Russia-India relations are currently improving, but the two countries have many challenges to deal with, especially the geopolitical interests that are dragging India to turn to the United States to counter the influence. China, an ally of Russia. So how will these issues that Russia and India address address the region and global politics?
The rise of better relations between the United States and India has kept Russia unhappy for years. According to Chinese experts, this is one of the reasons for India to flee to the United States, and its accession to the United States, Australia and Japan has become more pronounced of anti-Chinese tendencies combined with border disputes with China. Leave space for Washington to shake hands with India.
But whether it wants to or not, India must maintain good relations with a great power like Russia, and the purchase of weapons from Russia could be an effort that New Delhi wants to appease Moscow and say Russia is still important to India. On the other hand, India and Russia are also members of a number of alliances, the Brics and the Shanghai Cooperation Organization, all of which provide a means for the two countries to work together.
* Trade and weapons
Russia is looking to increase arms exports to India, and major deals could be announced after the meeting between Putin and Modi.
