The seven rays of the sun travel in the form of waves of different lengths and lengths. Among them, blue light is shorter than red light. Remember that light travels in a straight line unless something hits its path. For example, glass will destroy it, crystals like "prism" will bend, and gas molecules in the atmosphere will absorb and scatter them.
As the sun enters the atmosphere of the planet, it will be absorbed by gas molecules and tiny particles in the air and scatter all over the sky. Of the seven rays, short and small wavelengths, such as blue and purple, are captured and scatter more than any other color. But because our eyes are so stimulated with blue rather than purple, that is why we see the sky blue.
When the sun goes down in the sky, its light must pass through a dense atmosphere to reach us. As a result, the blue light is scattering more and more, and the red and yellow rays pass through our own eyes.
