The Banking Association of Cambodia highlights key challenges hindering access to credit for SMEs and women entrepreneurs
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Bun Yin, Vice President of the Banking Association of Cambodia, mentioned some of the main challenges that hinder the access of credit to small and medium enterprises and women entrepreneurs from banking and microfinance institutions. These include the lack of specific and accurate financial statements and the unregistered registration of businesses of small and medium enterprises and women entrepreneurs. These factors are the main reasons for hindering the provision of credit.
Mr. Bun Yin made the remarks at a seminar on the challenges and solutions of access to credit for small and medium enterprises and women entrepreneurs. This workshop is organized by the Banking Association of Cambodia and the Cambodian Microfinance Association, as well as Pact and many other partners.
He said that many small and medium enterprises and women entrepreneurs now have access to official financial services and the use of financial services responsibly and can contribute to the socio-economic development of families and communities. . These lead to a better life, business expansion opportunities, savings and financial preparedness.
He noted that "small and medium enterprises and women entrepreneurs still face problems with access to credit from banking and financial institutions, the main problems hindering access to credit, lack of accurate and accurate financial statements and non-compliance." "Official list of businesses and many other issues."
He added that today's workshop will participate in solving and exploring ways to solve the problem of obtaining credit from the banking and financial sector and provide a lot of information to the public, business people, etc. to understand the credit services and products. Finance.
In addition to the challenges of hindering access to credit for small and medium enterprises and women entrepreneurs, Ms. Neang Sovath Tha, Project Manager of Young Entrepreneurs of Cambodia, also mentioned some other points that lead to small and medium enterprises and Women entrepreneurs who do not receive credit include:
1. The company does not have proper records, financial statements and business plan. 2. The company does not have collateral, so it can not take a loan with a good interest rate. Item 4: Ability to repay debts and complexity of financing obligations.
Kea Boran, Chairman of the Board of the Microfinance Association of Cambodia, also highlighted the importance of the participation of small and medium enterprises and the role of women entrepreneurs who have contributed to the Cambodian national economy.
He added that 20.9 percent was used for trade and commerce, 15.9 percent was used for services and 19.3 percent was used for agriculture. Among them, women entrepreneurs have a high success rate compared to men, especially financial management. Russian President Vladimir Putin begins visit to India with a view to strengthening military and energy ties
