US passes new law that could force Chinese companies to leave US stock exchange
WASHINGTON (Reuters) - US market regulators have announced the passage of a law that would authorize regulators to remove foreign companies from the US stock market if they fail to provide information to auditors with a focus on companies. China. According to the AFP news agency.
The new law requires companies to disclose information on whether they are owned or controlled by the government, the AFP news agency reported.
Last year, the US Congress passed a law specifically targeting Chinese companies under which the Listed Companies Accounting Board (PCAOB) has the power to audit foreign companies listed on the US stock exchange.
The new law also requires companies to name any Chinese Communist Party members on their boards.
Beijing has refused to allow PCAOB to conduct audit inspections of companies registered in China and Hong Kong.
