According to Xinhua, the railway has 11 stations on the Chinese side and 10 on the Lao side, with a total length of 409 km. At 160 kilometers per hour, the train will reduce the travel time between Vientiane and the Sino-Lao border from two days to just three hours, and travel from Vientiane to Kunming can be costly. Only one night.
Xu Liping, director of the Center for Southeast Asian Studies at the Chinese Academy of Social Sciences, predicted that most of the trains would carry cargo in the early stages of its operation due to the Kovid-19 epidemic, which stalled international travel. .
"Goods, including raw materials and fresh agricultural products, can be shipped to China and then to Europe via the rail network, which will inject a new impetus not only into the domestic economy," he said. "But also to other countries in the region, such as Thailand, Malaysia, Singapore and Myanmar."
China's real estate market will continue to decline until the first half of 2022 due to tightening policy
BEIJING: China's real estate sector slump is expected to continue in the first half of 2022, with house prices and sales falling as tighter credit policies hit demand. According to Reuters.
The real estate sector, one of the main drivers of China's economic growth, has shrunk in recent months due to stricter regulations and a liquidity crisis that has engulfed the largest and most indebted real estate developers. Some of China.
According to a survey conducted by Reuters, home and property investment forecasts are gloomy than in August.
The average house price is estimated to fall 1 percent in the first half of the year, according to 14 analysts surveyed by Reuters from November 26 to December 1, 2022. 2022.
On the demand side, real estate sales by area are projected to decline 16 percent in the first half of 2022, up from 27.7 percent in the same period this year.
Supply-side expectations are dull, with real estate investment projected to fall 3 percent in the first half of 2022, up from 15 percent in the same period this year.
According to Reuters, the rapid downturn in the real estate sector has fueled speculation that policymakers could begin to ease restrictions on buyers and developers and even lower rates. Interest, too, if economic growth loses momentum.
But most observers expect the authorities to adhere to the same restrictions for now, despite some adjustments, including a easing of credit policy.
