(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Commerce has issued a notification on the extension of fines for applying for e-commerce licenses or licenses. This is according to the announcement that Fresh News received on December 2, 2021.
The Ministry of Commerce has confirmed that in accordance with the Notification No. 2194, dated 15 September 2021 on the issuance of e-commerce licenses or licenses, the application deadline will be on the 1st day. December 2021 is coming.
But so far, the Ministry of Commerce has observed that some enterprises and companies that have been conducting e-commerce operations are preparing documents to apply for e-commerce licenses or licenses.
Therefore, in order to enable the fulfillment of the obligation to apply for a license or e-commerce license, the Ministry of Commerce has decided to extend the fine date for this application until March 1, 2022.
The Ministry of Commerce strongly believes that during this period, all owners of sole proprietors, legal entities and affiliates of foreign companies who have been doing e-commerce will rush to apply for a business license or license. Electronic system by default.
(Kampong Cham): The Governor of Kampong Cham Province, Mr. Un Chanda, on December 2, 2021, assigned the Technical Officer of the Provincial Investment Planning Office to inspect and start the process of repairing some roads in Kampong Cham City which were damaged by the recent floods. Go.
Mr. Doul Son, Technical Officer of Kampong Cham Provincial Investment Planning Office, said that after Mr. Un Chanda, Provincial Governor, chaired a meeting to discuss the repair of roads and drainage in Boeung Kok, he ordered the technical team to inspect. Road repair and drainage works to release rainwater.
The United Nations Office for the Coordination of Humanitarian Affairs (OCHA) estimates that a total of 274 million people worldwide will need some form of emergency assistance by 2022. This figure is a 17% increase from the record high already in 2021.
A total of $ 41 billion will be needed next year, according to UN agencies and other humanitarian organizations to provide assistance to an estimated 183 million vulnerable people in 63 countries. This is up from the $ 35 billion proposed for 2021.
The UN report highlights the tragic nature of the growing demand for humanitarian aid caused by conflict and instability in countries such as Afghanistan, Ethiopia and Myanmar. Natural disasters and climate change, as well as the global epidemic crisis COVID-19, are also driving demand.
