The day was held in the presence of Mr. Pin Prak, Deputy Governor of Siem Reap, and Dr. Chhim Sarath, Director of the Asia Regional Office of the AIDS Care Foundation, with the participation of many young people.
AHF, along with governments and civil society organizations from around the world, has organized a number of commemorative events, including live events and social events, to maintain a safe distance to commemorate those who have died of HIV / AIDS and to raise public awareness. About the importance of blood tests, prevention and treatment of HIV and to seek support in continuing this important fight.
AIDS continues to claim nearly 700,000 lives each year worldwide. As world leaders have a duty to fight COVID-19, including a commitment to action to ensure access to vaccines for all, they must continue to fight the AIDS epidemic. By encouraging HIV testing and providing antiretroviral drugs to those in need.
AHF will host a variety of on-site and online events in its 45 member countries. These events aim to divert international attention to the HIV crisis, as well as to carriers and victims of the disease worldwide. The other is to serve as a reminder of the importance of access to testing, prevention and treatment of HIV.
"The World AIDS Day provides an opportunity to reflect on that progress and remind everyone that millions of people, especially vulnerable people, still do not have access to life-saving drugs," Sarath said. They are yet. Although Kovid 19 is still a major epidemic, world leaders must continue to work hard to end the AIDS crisis.
