(Preah Sihanouk): Mr. Net Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesman for the Ministry of Environment, said that the Royal Government of Cambodia, through the Ministry of Environment, plans to establish 5 or 6 standard landfill sites in some provinces in In 2022, at the same time, a standard landfill will be put into operation in Sihanoukville.
While the standard parallel ground has been decided by the Preah Sihanouk Provincial Administration to put in place a new landfill in O Ta Sek Village, O Oknha Heng Commune, Prey Nob District, Preah Sihanouk Province, about 3 km down from National Road 4 after construction. Pits in the first phase in early November 2021. While the first standard landfill cost more than $ 5 million to build in the national budget.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra added that the goal of the Ministry of Environment is to manage solid waste well, which is part of the national policy that the Royal Government has implemented, and most importantly, the promotion as well as the provision of technical assistance to the lower level administration. National in Urban Waste Management.
In addition, the senior official highlighted: "Every day, more than 10,000 tons of waste is generated, and 4 million tons of waste per year. Separately, in Sihanoukville, between 350 and 400 tons of garbage is generated per day. "According to the study, 65 percent of this waste is organic waste, 20 percent is plastic waste and the rest is solid waste."
Mr. Neth Pheaktra confirmed that the Ministry of Environment has pushed for the processing and removal of waste according to its type. Currently, according to the Ministry's policy, people are required to sort garbage to reduce the amount of garbage to be dumped on the landfill, which reduces the amount and will help the landfill to last longer.
"There are four landfills in Sihanoukville on five hectares of land with a five-year landfill project, but this may also change depending on the growth of the landfill," he said.
He added that currently about 10 percent of the garbage is recycled and the rest is dumped in the field. But in other countries, people who are aware of waste sorting are less likely to dump it at landfills, and some countries have almost no more.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra stated, "This is the first landfill in Cambodia that has been established with the right standard, while in 2022, the Royal Government plans to establish in other provinces such as Kep, Kampong Chhnang." Pursat, Battambang, Banteay Meanchey, Svay Rieng and so on.
Around this case, the Secretary of State added that in a year, the increase of garbage by more than 10 percent due to economic growth and consumption and population growth. In case of uncontrolled solid waste, garbage will affect the health, beauty and environment.
The spokesman added that Sihanoukville is the focus of the Royal Government in the management of waste because it is a multi-purpose province in the areas of industry, ports, airports, tourism and so on.
In particular, according to Mr. Hok Sophannaro, Managing Director of the Sihanoukville Urban Waste Management Landfill, said that the Otasek landfill in Sihanoukville consumes 250 tons of waste per day, and this waste is not But from Sihanoukville, it also comes from Prey Nob district, which operates two companies.
Mr. Hok Sokandaro said that the landfill covers a total area of more than 50 hectares, but for the first phase, it will cover an area of 17 hectares. He said that the garbage stored at the new location was collected from Sihanoukville and Prey Nup districts, with two garbage collection and transportation companies after the pits at the previous location were full.
He said that for the landfill in Sihanoukville, in addition to the standard garbage disposal, they still make the right distribution, in which each type of waste can be recycled for each type of it.
According to a report by the Ministry of Public Works and Transport, the landfill in Sihanoukville is under construction on a 51-hectare site with a total national budget of more than $ 5.31 million.
In the first five years, four pits were dug, with an average width of 130 meters and a length of 150 meters, a depth of 2-4 meters, and a rainwater harvesting system from the bottom of the pit. Dirt from landfills and exhaust pipes.
