Minister of Labor Supports Helen Keller Organizing Pilot Project for Health and Nutrition Promotion in Factories
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Ith Sam Heng, Minister of Labor and Vocational Training, supported Helen Keller, which will be piloting health and nutrition projects in a number of factories, requesting a Memorandum of Understanding (MoU). Together between the Ministry of Labor and the organization to make this project effective and successful.
The Minister's support was given on the occasion of allowing Ms. Gwyneth Cotes, Country Director of Helen Keller International Cambodia to pay a courtesy call on the Ministry of Labor on the morning of November 30, 2021, requesting the cooperation of the Ministry of Labor. On piloting health and nutrition promotion projects in some factories.
During the meeting, the Minister praised Helen Keller, which has been active since 1992, and has made significant contributions to the socio-economic development of Cambodia. The policy of the Royal Government of Cambodia considers NGOs to be an indispensable social partner, and this partnership between the government and non-governmental organizations will definitely help further the sustainability of the organization's activities.
He also mentioned the Kovid-19 crisis, which has hampered all sectors, especially the labor sector, which is facing difficulties, causing workers to lose their jobs and incomes to make ends meet, which requires the government. Efforts are being made to address the issue through package support, but the Royal Government of Cambodia has selected a vaccination strategy as a key to reassuring people in the community and reopening the country.
Ms. Gwyneth Cotes informed the Minister that Helen Keller is a non-governmental humanitarian organization that is implementing health, food security and nutrition promotion programs in Cambodia in close collaboration with the Council for Agricultural and Rural Development. Minister of Health, Ministry of Commerce, Ministry of Planning, Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports and other relevant ministries and institutions.
Helen Keller is currently piloting a project to improve the health and nutrition of women and children in a number of factories through the GIZ-MUSEFO project funding to contribute to the health, productivity and economy of Cambodia.
The Minister mentioned the care of the workers from the head of the Royal Government, Samdech Techo Prime Minister visited the factory in the past, and Samdech Techo recommended to be organized. Work must have a canteen, breastfeeding place and so on. In addition, this project is also a message to the major international orders market about the protection of working conditions and the harmonization of industrial relations in Cambodia.
The Minister stated that the project is done in the factory, not in the community, so the factory location is a business place that is managed by the employer, have clear working hours, be sure to have good professional relations with stakeholders. He proposed a formal cooperation with a memorandum between the organization and the Ministry to have activities to implement and have a clear goal to achieve better results.
She thanked the Minister for the cooperation that led to the signing of the agreement to implement the project and hoped that the project to promote health and nutrition in these factories will be successful.
