GENEVA, Nov. 28 (Xinhua) - All member states of the World Health Organization (WHO) have unanimously agreed to negotiate a future agreement on communicable disease prevention, international diplomats said on Sunday. Outbreaks that will reduce differences between the EU and the United States. This is according to the release by the Chinese newspaper CGTN on Monday evening, November 29, 2021.
The draft, released at last weekend's talks, will be presented for approval to health ministers at a three-day WHO special session, which opens Monday. This November 2021 (Geneva time).
The Global Agreement to Strengthen the Prevention and Response to Epidemics, which is expected to be completed by May 2024, will cover topics such as sharing data and genetic sequencing of viruses, as well as vaccines; Any potential drugs obtained from research.
The United Kingdom, along with the European Union and 70 other countries, has pushed for a binding treaty. The United States, along with Brazil and India, has been reluctant to commit to the treaty, diplomats said last week. The draft has already been posted on the World Health Organization website.
