WHO warns Cambodia to be prepared for a possible increase in 19 cases of Kovid after the world discovered a new Kovid 19 mutation called Omikrong
(Phnom Penh): The representative of the World Health Organization in Cambodia on November 27, 2021, reminded Cambodia to be ready for the eventual increase of 19 cases of Kovid after the world discovered 19 Kovid. Transform an alarming newcomer named Omi Krong.
Omega is a new mutant virus of concern for the global response to the Kovid 19 pandemic. Omegon can mutate into a large number of viruses, and some of these mutations cause concern. Preliminary evidence suggests that the new mutation virus poses a higher risk than other alarming new mutations in Kovid-19 infection.
Omega is also being detected at a faster rate than previous rates of infection, suggesting that the new mutation virus could easily infect humans and make it more susceptible to the virus. Infected more than the previous new mutation virus. We do not know the effect of the vaccine on this new mutated virus. Researchers are examining the effects of the new mutation virus on vaccines and other devices.
Scientists are also working to find out more about the mutations and the effects of the new virus, how strong it is and how fast it can spread.
The representative of the World Health Organization in Cambodia added, however, that the positive news is that the analysis of Kovid-19 virus using PCR laboratory machines today continues to detect the new mutant virus, so we Can monitor its spread. We can refine and strengthen our response to Kovid-19 based on new information to reduce its threat.
The World Health Organization (WHO) continues to assess the rapidly evolving situation, monitor the emergence of the new mutant virus and its spread, and advise on measures to respond to the threat of the mutation virus. The Kovid-19 epidemic is not over. Cambodia has been making every effort to save lives and protect health as Cambodia embarks on a safe and sustainable reopening process.
