A number of countries, including the United States, China and Japan, have jointly released oil from national strategic stocks to lower global crude oil prices (Video inside) (Washington): A number of countries, including the United States, China and Japan, have decided to release crude oil from their strategic stocks. In a bid to bring down the world's most recent high oil prices and curb rising inflation.
The release comes after OPEC member states and its allies have repeatedly rejected requests by the United States and other oil-using countries to pump more crude oil in response to the crisis. Rising demand.
Earlier in the day, oil prices hit a seven-year high of more than $ 86 a barrel.
The United States later asked China, India, South Korea and Japan to release strategic oil reserves, working with the United States.
Following the request, some countries decided to release their oil reserves, and the United States itself decided to sell its oil.
The United States has confirmed the sale of 32 million barrels of crude oil from four strategic oil reserves (SPR) to be delivered by the end of December 2021 and April 2022, according to Reuters. Try to lower world oil prices.
Other countries include:
"We have been working with the United States to stabilize the international oil market and we have decided to join the United States in selling parts of our national oil reserves in a way that will "It is not contrary to the Japanese reserve oil law in force."
China's Strategic Food and Petroleum Administration confirmed to Reuters last week that it was working on the release of the stockpile, but declined to comment on the US proposal.
However, Chinese Foreign Ministry spokesman Zhao Lijian told a news conference on Wednesday that "China will arrange for the release of state oil reserves according to the situation and needs and take other necessary measures to maintain market stability." "Oil." "China has long been concerned about the stability of the international oil market, and we are ready to maintain contact with stakeholders on market balance and long-term stability," he added.
India India has agreed to release 5 million barrels of oil.
According to Canada's ctvnews.com, the government said in a statement: "India has repeatedly expressed concern about oil supplies being kept below artificial levels by oil-producing countries. "Which leads to higher prices and negative consequences."
South Korea ctvnews.com reports that the South Korean government said in a statement that the amount and timing of oil spills will be decided in consultation with other countries, but the country expects That will be similar to the case of international cooperation in the past.
During the crisis in Libya in 2011, when the civil war disrupted oil supplies, South Korea released nearly 3.5 million barrels of crude oil, or about 4 percent of the country's reserves.
According to ctvnews.com, the British government said in a statement that it would allow companies to voluntarily release up to 1.5 million barrels of oil.
"As we have said before, we will work closely with our international partners to do what we can to support the global economy," a British government spokesman said.
