Three Villages in Banteay Srei District, Siem Reap Province Receive Paved Roads from the Royal Government
Banteay Srei District Governor Khim Finang said that the villagers of Sre Changhot, Thnal and Tani villages of Run Ta Ek commune had to endure the noise of the red gravel road, which was always very dusty, difficult to breathe and full of dust. Hold a thick blanket on the roof of each of their houses in the past.
Through Facebook, Mr. Khim Finang confirmed that when he arrived in these villages, especially Sre Changhot village, when he first came to fulfill his role in Banteay Srei district, he also saw the lively activities and the great potential. The tourism side of such an ancient village as well. All these villages are ancient villages with their own identity and rich traditions, not inferior to the village of Preah Dak, but it is disturbed by land issues.
The district governor's hopes have come true, although there are still roads through many villages that are still red gravel roads, but the fruits of progress that the Royal Government has been striving for over the years. Made such a project appear for the first time in the countryside.
Mr. Khim Finang believes that this paved road will be an important foundation for paving the way for local authorities to organize these villages with tourism value and attractiveness to push for further development. The next level will capture the potential of the area to the fullest to improve the daily lives of the people here.
Pailin longan farmers call for speeding up talks to export directly to China next year
(Phnom Penh): Pailin longan farmers have requested the Royal Government to expedite the negotiation of export procedures with the Chinese side to solve market problems for farmers, as this year's Pailin longan harvest season, farmers are facing losses. Markets and prices plummet.
He said that during the Pailin lentil harvest this year, prices have fallen sharply despite Thai opening of imports. He said that at present, the price of a kilogram of Pailin longan is between 1,000 riel and 1,200 riel for Cambodian traders who come to the plantation, and 1,500 riel for good fruit for export to Thailand, different from last year, which can be sold between 2,000 to 3,000 riel per kilo.
He expects that next year Mien Pailin will be able to export directly to China without going through Thailand as it is now to solve farmers' market problems and get value added.
"If we can export next year, it will be much easier because in Pailin we already have a kiln and the processing place for export is ready." "If exports are opened, it will be much better. It eases market congestion every day. We are completely dependent on them and because the domestic market is hopeless, it fluctuates." Mr. Tom Sophoan added.
According to the administrative officer of the Mien Pailin Association, in Pailin province, there are more than 200 families growing Mien Pailin and more than 2,000 hectares of cultivated land.
He said that negotiations on the conditions to meet the phytosanitary requirements to be able to import longan to the Chinese market have reached the stage of negotiating the list of pesticides by both sides. After this stage, the two sides will discuss and prepare a technical protocol, which will cover how to inspect and standardize plantations, packing facilities, processing facilities and disinfection facilities.
"I do not dare to conclude that, but we will try to speed up the negotiations, because this also depends on the Chinese side, sometimes it takes a long time to prepare documents, sometimes it takes time to send officials to inspect. "Sometimes he needs procedures to check online."
"We will negotiate a package on the issue of direct import of Cambodian agricultural products to China," he said. His Excellency Li Keqiang, he is interested in Tanen Mien Pailin will provide online monitoring features. In the past, I had to send my bodyguards and my money to buy and distribute to people in hospitals. "The Chinese prime minister has said he will buy directly from Cambodia without going through a third country."
